  :adr/body "# ADR 2799007542: cloud-itonami ISCO-08 7542 Shotfirers and Blasters blast-operation scheduling/logistics coordination coverage\n\n## Status\n\nAccepted\n\n## Context\n\nThis is part of the occupation-classification track (sibling to the\n`cloud-itonami-isic-*` industry-actor rollout, same governing\nADR-2607121000): codify every ISCO-08 occupation unit group as an\nautonomous \"actor\" (LLM/advisor behind an independent Governor, a\n`langgraph.graph` StateGraph, append-only audit ledger), modeled on a\nrobotics premise -- a robot performs the occupation's physical/\nadministrative work, proposing actions to an independent Governor that\ngates them, never dispatches hardware itself, always escalates\nhigh-stakes decisions to a human. This ADR covers **ISCO-08 class 7542\n-- Shotfirers and Blasters** only, Wave3 (production/trades) of the\nrollout (Wave0/1/2 100% complete, Wave3 batches 1-10 already landed:\n60 occupations so far).\n\nThe `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry's live `{:id \"7542\" ...}` entry\nwas verified against a fresh clone before any work began (`clojure -M\n-e \"(require 'kotoba.occupation) (println (kotoba.occupation/get-\noccupation \\\"7542\\\"))\"` from a checkout with the `kotoba-lang/\ntechnology` sibling present at the correct `../technology` relative\npath), and its `:name` read exactly \"Shotfirers and Blasters\" --\nconfirming the premise, no mismatch. Prior to this ADR the entry's\n`:maturity` was `:spec` with `:required-technologies [:robotics\n:identity :audit-ledger]`, `:optional-technologies []` and\n`:operating-states [:intake :propose :approve :execute :audit]`, and\nno `:repo`/`:business-id`; no repository existed at `cloud-itonami/\ncloud-itonami-isco-7542` (`gh api` 404 confirmed before scaffolding).\nThis ADR and its companion registry edit promote the `7542` entry to\n`:implemented`.\n\n**This actor carries an explosives-handling life-safety-critical\nhazard framing -- alongside aviation (ISCO 7232) and diving (ISCO\n7541) as the highest-stakes hazard framing established in this\ncatalog so far.** Shotfirers and blasters handle explosives for\ncontrolled demolition, mining and quarrying blasting operations --\nerrors can cause death, serious injury or major property destruction,\ncategorically higher-stakes than ordinary workshop trades. The closed\nop allowlist therefore must NEVER include any op that directly\nfinalizes a blast-authorization decision (approving a detonation to\nproceed) or a blast-execution decision, nor override a blasting\nsupervisor's/site-safety-officer's judgment -- these are always a\nhard, permanent, structural block (absent from the allowlist entirely,\nnot merely gated), never auto-commit-eligible under any confidence\nlevel. Any safety/anomaly flag on a blast operation (misfire,\nexclusion-zone breach, explosive-storage condition) ALWAYS escalates,\nno exceptions, ever.\n\n**This actor coordinates BLAST-OPERATION SCHEDULING/LOGISTICS ONLY --\nit never handles explosives itself and never makes a\nblast-authorization decision.** It is not an authority to decide when\nor whether a blast happens; it is a coordination layer for blast-log/\nprogress record logging, crew/site scheduling, safety-concern\nsurfacing and blasting-equipment/administrative-supply order\ncoordination (never explosives themselves -- explosives procurement/\nhandling is entirely out of scope for this administrative-coordination\nactor), all gated behind an independent governor. It never finalizes a\nblast-authorization decision and never performs or completes a blast.\n\nThe reference implementation mirrored for module SHAPE and the\nclosed-allowlist + independently-verified-provenance safety pattern is\n[`cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7232`](https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7232)\n(Aircraft Engine Mechanics and Repairers: `store`/`advisor`/\n`governor`/`actor` split, `langgraph.graph` StateGraph, full doc set,\nsame wave and the closest comparable life-safety-critical hazard\npattern already established in this fleet), cloned and read in full\nthis session.\n\n## Decision\n\nPublish `cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7542` as an OSS\ndocumentation/coordination actor for shotfirers and blasters,\nfollowing the SAME governed-actor architecture as every prior\n`cloud-itonami-isco-*` actor in this fleet: **Blast-Operation\nScheduling & Logistics Coordination Advisor ⊣ BlastCoordGovernor** on\na `langgraph.graph` StateGraph (`io.github.kotoba-lang/langgraph`,\ngit-sha-pinned identically to the reference's `deps.edn` -- a\ndifferent library coordinate from the `langgraph-clj` used by the\nunrelated `20-actors/*` actor family), with an independent Governor,\nhuman-in-the-loop approval via `interrupt-before`, and an append-only\naudit ledger. All source is portable `.cljc` (no JVM interop); tests\nare `clojure.test` `.clj`, matching the reference repo's own layout.\n\n### Closed proposal-op allowlist (all `:effect :propose`)\n\n- `:log-work-record` -- blast-log/progress data logging\n  (administrative metadata only)\n- `:schedule-crew-operation` -- crew/site/task scheduling proposal\n- `:flag-safety-concern` -- surface a misfire/exclusion-zone-breach/\n  explosive-storage-condition concern, ALWAYS escalates, NEVER\n  auto-commit-eligible, no confidence-level exception, ever\n- `:coordinate-supply-order` -- blasting-equipment/administrative-\n  supply procurement proposal (NOT explosives themselves --\n  explosives procurement/handling is entirely out of scope for this\n  administrative-coordination actor), escalates above a registered\n  cost threshold\n\nNo op in this allowlist finalizes a blast-authorization decision\n(approving a detonation to proceed), finalizes a blast-execution\ndecision, or overrides a blasting supervisor's/site-safety-officer's\njudgment -- these decision classes are structurally absent from the\nallowlist, not merely gated behind escalation.\n\n### Six HARD governor checks (`blastcoord.governor`, permanent, never\noverridable by human approval)\n\n1. **no-site** -- the blaster/site record must be independently\n   verified/registered before any action.\n2. **no-actuation** -- proposal `:effect` must be `:propose` (the\n   governor never dispatches hardware and never handles explosives).\n3. **unknown-op** -- `:op` must be one of the four allowlisted\n   coordination ops; the closed op-allowlist is enforced\n   unconditionally.\n4. **site-mismatch** -- if the proposal names a site, it must be the\n   SAME site verified for this request (defense-in-depth against a\n   proposal quietly targeting an unverified site).\n5. **unknown-blaster / blaster-wrong-site** -- if the proposal\n   references a blaster, that blaster must be a REGISTERED certified\n   shotfirer/blaster belonging to this site.\n6. **scope-exclusion-violation** -- a proposal that attempts, in any\n   free-text field, to authorize a blast to proceed, to finalize a\n   blast-execution decision, or to override a blasting supervisor's or\n   site-safety-officer's judgment is a HARD, PERMANENT block,\n   evaluated unconditionally on content, independent of `:op`.\n\n### Escalation gates, plus the general confidence floor\n\n- `:flag-safety-concern` -- ALWAYS escalates to a human, regardless of\n  confidence (`blastcoord.governor/always-escalate-ops`). This is the\n  ONLY channel by which the robot may surface a misfire/exclusion-\n  zone-breach/explosive-storage-condition concern -- it never resolves\n  the concern itself, and this is unconditional: no confidence level,\n  however high, ever exempts a safety flag from human sign-off.\n- a `:coordinate-supply-order` proposal whose `:cost` exceeds\n  `supply-order-cost-threshold` (20000, inclusive boundary verified by\n  `ok-supply-order-at-or-below-cost-threshold`) -- always needs human\n  sign-off, independent of confidence.\n- advisor confidence below `confidence-floor` (0.6) also escalates,\n  for any op.\n\n### Self-trip guard (fleet-known bug class)\n\nA governor's scope-exclusion term list phrased as bare nouns (e.g.\n\"explosive\", \"blast\", \"detonation\") can accidentally match inside the\nmock advisor's own default rationale text for a legitimate proposal,\nself-blocking the actor's own happy path -- this risk is especially\nsharp here because this actor's ENTIRE domain is blast-operation\ncoordination, so bare nouns like \"explosive\"/\"blast\"/\"detonation\"/\n\"shot\"/\"round\" are ordinary, unavoidable vocabulary.\n`blastcoord.governor/scope-exclusion-phrases` is phrased exclusively\nas finalization/execution ACTION PHRASES (e.g. \"authorize the blast to\nproceed\", \"finalize the blast operation\", \"override the blasting\nsupervisor's judgment\"), never bare nouns, and\n`blastcoord.governor-test/default-mock-advisor-proposals-never-self-trip-scope-exclusion`\nis a dedicated regression test asserting every op in the closed\nallowlist, driven through the default mock advisor (including a\n`:flag-safety-concern` request whose description names an \"unresolved\nmisfire concern near bench 4 round 12, exclusion-zone breach pending\nreview\"), never trips the scope-exclusion guard. A companion test,\n`scope-exclusion-not-triggered-by-bare-domain-nouns`, asserts directly\nagainst `governor/scope-exclusion-violation?` that a rationale/\ndescription containing only bare domain nouns (\"proposed schedule-\ncrew-operation for round 12 blast preparation on site BS-1\", \"blaster\nfully certified for explosive storage handling and detonation\nprocedure documentation for this shot\") does not trip the guard, while\n`hard-and-permanent-on-scope-exclusion-authorize-blast-to-proceed`,\n`hard-and-permanent-on-scope-exclusion-finalize-blast-operation`,\n`hard-and-permanent-on-scope-exclusion-finalize-detonation-authorization`,\n`hard-and-permanent-on-scope-exclusion-override-blasting-supervisor`\nand\n`hard-and-permanent-on-scope-exclusion-override-site-safety-officer`\nconfirm the actual forbidden action phrases DO trip it, and\n`holds-a-scope-excluded-proposal-with-no-interrupt-path`,\n`holds-a-finalize-blast-operation-proposal-with-no-interrupt-path` and\n`holds-an-override-blasting-supervisor-proposal-with-no-interrupt-path`\nconfirm the full actor graph resolves such attempts straight to\n`:hold` (a finish point, never `:request-approval`) with no record\ncommitted.\n\n## Consequences\n\n- `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry's `{:id \"7542\" ...}` entry\n  promoted from `:spec` to `:implemented`, with `:repo`/`:business-id`\n  set to the real repository\n  (`https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7542` /\n  `cloud-itonami-isco-7542`), and `maturity-summary`'s implemented\n  count bumped accordingly (recomputed live from the registry via\n  `kotoba.occupation/maturity-summary`, not assumed, immediately\n  before the edit to account for concurrent sibling landings in this\n  same batch).\n- Full test suite (`clojure -M:test`): 27 tests, 69 assertions, 0\n  failures, 0 errors -- raw output captured in this ADR's\n  `:test/counts` and in the task report, independently re-verified\n  against a fresh clone of the pushed repository (mojibake scan: 0\n  replacement characters found).\n- `clojure -M:lint` (clj-kondo): 0 errors, 0 warnings.\n- No `manifest/west.yml` changes -- this actor is registered purely\n  via the `kotoba-lang/occupation` capability registry, consistent\n  with sibling `cloud-itonami-isco-*`/`cloud-itonami-isic-*` actors.\n- No `kotoba-lang/technology` dependency was added to the actor's\n  `deps.edn` -- only the central `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry\n  itself depends on it.\n- This ADR is EDN-only, matching the live `90-docs/adr` convention\n  (commit `6d2d0eeda658`) -- a single-entity EDN vector with an\n  `:adr/body` string blob rather than a paired `.md`+`.edn`.\n- Full doc set published: README.md, CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md,\n  CONTRIBUTING.md, GOVERNANCE.md, SECURITY.md, LICENSE\n  (AGPL-3.0-or-later, byte-identical to the `cloud-itonami-isco-7232`\n  reference's LICENSE text), docs/business-model.md,\n  docs/operator-guide.md, blueprint.edn.\n\n## Alternatives considered\n\n- Giving the actor an op like `:approve-blast-authorization` or\n  `:authorize-blast` that is merely gated behind `always-escalate-ops`:\n  rejected outright per the task's explicit explosives-safety guidance\n  -- an always-escalate op still means the *capability* to finalize a\n  blast-authorization decision exists in the code path and is merely\n  deferred to a human gate. For a decision that can directly cost\n  lives from an explosives-handling error with major property-\n  destruction potential, the capability itself must not exist in the\n  allowlist at all -- this matches the strict bar already established\n  by `cloud-itonami-isco-7232` (airworthiness-clearance/return-to-\n  service determinations) and `cloud-itonami-isco-7541`\n  (dive-authorization decisions).\n- A bare-noun scope-exclusion term list (e.g. `[\"explosive\" \"blast\"\n  \"detonation\"]`): rejected per the fleet-known self-trip bug class --\n  this actor's entire domain is blast-operation coordination, so bare\n  nouns would self-trip on essentially every legitimate proposal\n  (work-record logs naming \"round 12 progress notes\", crew-schedule\n  proposals naming \"exclusion-zone setup\", safety flags naming\n  \"misfire concern\", supply orders naming \"blasting mats and\n  administrative exclusion-zone signage\", etc.). Scope-exclusion terms\n  were phrased exclusively as multi-word finalization/execution ACTION\n  phrases instead, per the task's explicit self-tripping-bug-pattern\n  guidance.\n- Checking site/blaster provenance only from the proposal (never the\n  request): rejected -- the site-mismatch check requires the site\n  identity to be anchored at the request level and independently\n  cross-checked against anything the proposal claims, so a proposal\n  that quietly names a different, unverified site is caught even if\n  the proposal otherwise looks well-formed.\n- Modeling the blaster-registration check as unconditionally required\n  on every op: rejected as too rigid -- `:coordinate-supply-order`\n  legitimately has no blaster to reference (it is a materials-\n  procurement proposal against the site, not a person); the check\n  applies only when a proposal actually references a `:blaster-id`.\n- Allowing `:coordinate-supply-order` to cover explosives procurement:\n  rejected outright per the task's explicit scope guidance -- this\n  actor coordinates BLAST-OPERATION SCHEDULING/LOGISTICS ONLY, and\n  explosives procurement/handling is entirely out of scope; the op's\n  docstring and every fixture in this repo name only blasting-\n  equipment/administrative-supply items (blasting mats, exclusion-zone\n  signage), never explosives themselves.\n"}]
